SINA_SMART["hemato1-lymphadenopathy"] = {"lecture":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy","title":"Lymphadenopathy","mapStatus":"auto: generated by universal.py from the lecture summary and key points; not human-reviewed","los":[{"id":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e0","text":"Lymphadenopathy: node > 1 cm; inguinal up to 2 cm and soft submandibular < 1 cm may be normal.","topic":"Definition and fundamentals","sec":0,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e1","text":"Localized (1 area or 2 adjacent, ~3/4) vs generalized (≥ 2 non-contiguous, ~1/4).","topic":"Definition and fundamentals","sec":0,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e2","text":"< 1% malignant in primary care; lymphadenitis = tender, warm node.","topic":"Definition and fundamentals","sec":0,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e3","text":"Drainage: cervical head/neck; axillary arm/breast; inguinal leg/genitals/anus; supraclavicular thorax/abdomen.","topic":"Nodes, territories and mechanisms","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e4","text":"Mechanisms: lymphocyte proliferation, macrophages, lymphoma, metastasis.","topic":"Nodes, territories and mechanisms","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e5","text":"Mediastinal nodes → superior vena cava syndrome.","topic":"Clinical approach","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e6","text":"Key points: age, size, quality, time course, localized vs generalized.","topic":"Clinical approach","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e7","text":"Fistulising node → tuberculosis; hard or fixed → cancer.","topic":"Clinical approach","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e8","text":"Biopsy: hard/fixed, > 3 cm, > 4-6 weeks, or no diagnosis after 4 weeks.","topic":"Investigations and biopsy","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e10","text":"Generalized: mononucleosis, toxoplasmosis, HIV, phenytoin, carbamazepine, lupus; lymphoma, leukaemia.","topic":"Aetiologies","sec":4,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e9","text":"Right supraclavicular: lung, mediastinum; left: abdomen.","topic":"Aetiologies","sec":4,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"}],"vmap":{},"mlevel":{"1vr2i14":"a","1o5jkcx":"a","1pmcov1":"a","wyhnnt":"a","593g95":"a","502t3p":"a","16hyva5":"a","vgle4j":"a","61reqk":"a","1c4oup7":"a","1rwwzqo":"a","462e74":"a","1l79k0l":"a","jiwv6g":"a","1vazjpi":"a"},"mapCounts":{"verified":0,"auto":15,"ambiguous":0,"missing":0},"mrules":[],"items":[],"experimental":[],"status":{},"difficulty":{},"expl":{"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e0":{"s":0,"u":["Lymphadenopathy: node > 1 cm; inguinal up to 2 cm and soft submandibular < 1 cm may be normal.","Fundamentals: normal nodes are not palpable (nor in newborns); only cervical, axillary and inguinal areas (plus supraclavicular, epitrochlear, popliteal) are clinically accessible; soft flat submandibular nodes &lt; 1 cm and inguinal nodes up to 2 cm can be normal; fewer than 1% are malignant in primary care.","Lymphadenopathy (ADP) is enlargement of lymph nodes whatever the cause, usually over 1 cm ."],"dp":[]},"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e1":{"s":0,"u":["Localized (1 area or 2 adjacent, ~3/4) vs generalized (≥ 2 non-contiguous, ~1/4).","It is localized (one node area or two adjacent areas; about three quarters of cases in primary care) or generalized (two or more non-contiguous areas; about a quarter)."],"dp":[]},"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e2":{"s":0,"u":["< 1% malignant in primary care; lymphadenitis = tender, warm node.","Lymphadenitis means an infected or inflamed node: tender, painful, warm.","Fundamentals: normal nodes are not palpable (nor in newborns); only cervical, axillary and inguinal areas (plus supraclavicular, epitrochlear, popliteal) are clinically accessible; soft flat submandibular nodes &lt; 1 cm and inguinal nodes up to 2 cm can be normal; fewer than 1% are malignant in primary care."],"dp":[]},"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e3":{"s":1,"u":["Drainage: cervical head/neck; axillary arm/breast; inguinal leg/genitals/anus; supraclavicular thorax/abdomen.","Always examine the territory a node drains, and the nodes draining any lesion: cervical → head, neck, thyroid, scalp; axillary → upper limb, breast; inguinal → lower limb, external genitalia, anal region, lower abdomen; supraclavicular → thorax and abdomen (stomach, pancreas, ovary, testis, kidney).","Accessible: cervical, supraclavicular, axillary, inguinal, popliteal, epitrochlear."],"dp":[]},"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e4":{"s":1,"u":["Mechanisms: lymphocyte proliferation, macrophages, lymphoma, metastasis.","Four mechanisms of enlargement: lymphocyte proliferation against an antigen (infection), macrophage accumulation (inflammatory diseases), malignant lymphocyte proliferation (lymphoma), metastatic colonisation by non-lymphoid cancer cells."],"dp":[]},"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e5":{"s":2,"u":["Mediastinal nodes → superior vena cava syndrome.","Deep nodes reveal themselves by compression : in the mediastinum, superior vena cava syndrome (swelling of face and neck, distended neck and chest veins, cough, dyspnoea, orthopnoea); in the abdomen, leg swelling, pain, jaundice, urinary obstruction."],"dp":[]},"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e6":{"s":2,"u":["Key points: age, size, quality, time course, localized vs generalized.","Five key points: age (children: mostly infection; adults: also cancer and autoimmune disease), size (&gt; 2 cm must be investigated), quality (painful with redness and warmth → infection; hard → possibly malignant; fixed → cancer or surrounding inflammation such as tuberculosis; fistulising → tuberculosis ), time course , and localized vs generalized ."],"dp":[]},"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e7":{"s":2,"u":["Fistulising node → tuberculosis; hard or fixed → cancer.","Five key points: age (children: mostly infection; adults: also cancer and autoimmune disease), size (&gt; 2 cm must be investigated), quality (painful with redness and warmth → infection; hard → possibly malignant; fixed → cancer or surrounding inflammation such as tuberculosis; fistulising → tuberculosis ), time course , and localized vs generalized ."],"dp":[]},"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e8":{"s":3,"u":["Biopsy: hard/fixed, > 3 cm, > 4-6 weeks, or no diagnosis after 4 weeks.","When to biopsy : a hard, irregular or fixed node; size &gt; 3 cm ; persistence &gt; 4-6 weeks ; or no diagnosis after 4 weeks of investigation."],"dp":[]},"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e10":{"s":4,"u":["Generalized: mononucleosis, toxoplasmosis, HIV, phenytoin, carbamazepine, lupus; lymphoma, leukaemia.","Generalized (CBC, ESR, CRP, imaging needed): with an infectious or inflammatory context: infectious mononucleosis, toxoplasmosis, HIV , drugs ( phenytoin, carbamazepine ), autoimmune diseases; without one: haematological malignancies (lymphomas, acute or chronic leukaemias)."],"dp":[]},"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e9":{"s":4,"u":["Right supraclavicular: lung, mediastinum; left: abdomen.","Localized, non-inflammatory : think cancer: cervical (head and neck, thyroid, scalp), right supraclavicular → lung and mediastinum , left supraclavicular → abdomen , inguinal (external genitalia, anus); always look at the skin for melanoma ."],"dp":[{"h":"Why a left supraclavicular node points to the abdomen","t":"Lymph from the abdomen and lower body ascends in the thoracic duct , which empties into the left venous angle, where the left subclavian and internal jugular veins meet, just behind the left supraclavicular nodes. Cancer cells travelling up the duct can lodge there, so an enlarged left supraclavicular node (Virchow's node, Troisier's sign) may be the first sign of a gastric, pancreatic, testicular or ovarian cancer. The right side drains the right thorax and lung through the right lymphatic duct, which is why right-sided nodes point to the chest. Source: the lecture's drainage table; the anatomy of the thoracic duct from Anatomy 1."}]}},"meta":{"1vr2i14":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e0/recall/1"},"1o5jkcx":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e1/recall/1"},"1pmcov1":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e2/recall/1"},"wyhnnt":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e3/recall/1"},"593g95":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e9/recall/1"},"502t3p":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e7/recall/1"},"16hyva5":{"a":"relationship","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e5/relationship/1"},"vgle4j":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e8/recall/1"},"61reqk":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e5/recall/1"},"1c4oup7":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e0/recall/2"},"1rwwzqo":{"a":"relationship","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e10/relationship/1"},"462e74":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e10/recall/1"},"1l79k0l":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e7/recall/2"},"jiwv6g":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e4/recall/1"},"1vazjpi":{"a":"recall","vg":"hemato1-lymphadenopathy:e9/recall/2"}},"explRev":"5f7ee234ddfd"};
